    gist: 'The family is the basic unit of society and its core job is to manage its resources (time, money, energy, skills) to meet members\' needs. For young children there are two kinds of need. PHYSICAL needs are the tangible essentials a child must have to grow: food (a healthy balanced diet that meets the child\'s nutritional requirements, limiting unhealthy food), clothing (sufficient clothing for warmth, modesty and protection) and shelter (a warm, safe home to live, rest and play in). PSYCHOLOGICAL needs are emotional: love and affection (which the child learns to transfer into their own future relationships), security and trust from a dependable, constant caregiver, praise and encouragement to build self-esteem and a stable home life, and protection from physical, emotional and sexual abuse. Meeting both well is what helps a child become a well-adjusted adult. Contemporary family structures (cohabitation, dual-career, one-parent, blended, same-sex families) are reshaped by social, economic and technological change, but the function of meeting needs stays the same.',
