    gist: 'Insulation works on one principle: materials that trap air and conduct heat poorly (air, polystyrene, fibreglass) prevent/reduce heat loss, so the home stays warm and uses less energy. Methods cover the whole house: ATTIC/roof (fibre blanket, loose-fill, foam, blown cellulose), WALLS (cavity walls filled with air/polystyrene sheets/foam/bonded beads, or solid-wall blanket/external insulation), FLOORS (sealing gaps around floorboards, thick-underlay fitted carpets), DOORS/WINDOWS (double or triple glazing, draught proofing, well-fitting frames, heavy lined curtains). A well-insulated home still needs adequate VENTILATION — to provide oxygen-rich fresh air, remove stale air, allow combustion, control humidity and prevent condensation, medical conditions and structural damage. Beyond insulation, energy efficiency comes from choosing A-rated appliances, not leaving things on standby, cold and full-load washes, LED lighting, zoned heating with thermostats and timers, and a serviced condensing boiler.',
