    id: 'he-marriage-breakdown-options',
    subjectId: 'home-economics', topicId: 'home-economics-2-1',
    subjectLabel: 'Home Economics', topicLabel: 'Marriage & the Family Life Cycle',
    focus: 'Legal options when a marriage breaks down', level: 'higher',
    gist: 'When a marriage breaks down, Irish law offers several distinct options and HL students must know how they differ. A deed of separation is agreed by the couple in mediation and drawn up by a solicitor — they agree to live apart and settle care of dependent children, maintenance, the family home and succession rights. A judicial separation is granted by a court when the couple cannot reach a separation agreement or only one spouse wishes to separate (grounds include adultery, desertion, unreasonable behaviour, or living apart) — the couple remain married. A legal nullity means the marriage is treated as never having validly existed, so the partners are free to marry again; neither party can claim maintenance or share in the other\'s estate. A divorce ends an existing valid marriage by court decree (spouses must have lived apart for at least two of the previous three years, with no prospect of reconciliation and adequate provision for spouses and dependent children) — it gives the right to remarry. The supporting sociology terms also appear in the same question: universality of the family (the family is present in virtually all known societies), kinship (relationships by blood, marriage or common ancestry) and monogamy (a relationship with only one partner at a time).',
