    focus: 'Vitamin C', level: 'ordinary',
    gist: 'Vitamin C (ascorbic acid) is a water-soluble vitamin found in fresh fruit and vegetables. Good sources are citrus fruit (oranges, lemons), berries, kiwi, peppers, broccoli and potatoes. Its main jobs in the body are: it helps form collagen for healthy gums, skin and connective tissue; it promotes the healing of wounds; it acts as an antioxidant; it helps the immune system fight infection; and it helps the body absorb iron from food (it converts non-haem iron into a form the body can use). Because it dissolves in water and is destroyed by heat, a lot of it is lost if vegetables are boiled in a lot of water for a long time, so cook quickly in a little water or steam. A shortage of Vitamin C in the diet causes scurvy (bleeding gums), slow wound healing, anaemia and a greater chance of getting sick.',
