    id: 'he-ol-food-spoilage',
    subjectId: 'home-economics', topicId: 'home-economics-0-2',
    subjectLabel: 'Home Economics', topicLabel: 'Food Science & Food Commodities',
    focus: 'Causes of food spoilage', level: 'ordinary',
    gist: 'Food spoilage means food going off so it is no longer safe or pleasant to eat. There are a few main causes. ENZYME ACTION: natural enzymes in the food keep working after harvest, causing fruit to over-ripen and cut surfaces (apples, potatoes) to go brown when exposed to air. MICROBIAL CONTAMINATION: micro-organisms — bacteria, moulds and yeasts — grow on the food and break it down. MOISTURE LOSS: water escapes through the skins of fruit and vegetables so they shrivel and wilt. (Chemical contamination, e.g. from pesticides, is also accepted.) Preservation works by attacking these causes: heat (boiling, canning, jam-making) destroys micro-organisms and denatures enzymes; drying and adding salt or sugar remove the water micro-organisms need (water passes out of the microbes by osmosis); freezing slows everything down; and the acid in vinegar (pickling) makes conditions micro-organisms cannot grow in.',
