    focus: 'Gearing & Debt/Equity Ratio', level: 'higher',
    gist: 'Gearing measures financial risk: how much of a company\'s funding comes from DEBT (long-term loans/debentures) versus EQUITY (issued/ordinary share capital + reserves/retained earnings). The Debt/Equity ratio = Debt ÷ Equity, written as a ratio out of 1 (e.g. 0.5:1). A company is HIGHLY geared when debt makes up MORE than half of total capital employed — i.e. the ratio is above 1:1, where debt exceeds equity. It is LOWLY geared when debt is below 50% of capital (ratio below 1:1). Highly geared = riskier, because loan interest must be paid even in a bad year, whereas dividends to shareholders are optional. At 0.5:1 a company is lowly geared.',
