      modelAnswer: '(a) Gearing is the amount of debt (long-term loans) a company uses to fund its operations in proportion to its equity (share capital + retained earnings); it is an indicator of financial risk. (b) Debt/Equity = Debt ÷ Equity = 300,000 ÷ (550,000 + 50,000) = 300,000 ÷ 600,000 = 0.5:1. (Authorised share capital of €900,000 is ignored.) At 0.5:1 the company is lowly geared.',
