    gist: 'Three key principles govern every insurance contract. Utmost Good Faith: the person taking out insurance must be truthful and declare ALL material facts (anything that affects the level of risk, e.g. a medical condition or a thatched house / house near a river) on the proposal form. Insurable Interest: you can only insure something you have a financial interest in — you would gain from its existence and suffer financially from its loss (you can insure your own house but have no insurable interest in your neighbour\'s). Indemnity: you cannot make a profit from insurance; a claim only restores you to the same financial position you were in before the loss (a car insured for €30,000 but with a market value of €20,000 pays out only €20,000 on a write-off).',
