    focus: 'Le passé composé vs l\'imparfait', level: 'higher',
    gist: 'Both are past tenses, but they do different jobs. The PASSÉ COMPOSÉ is for one completed action — what HAPPENED: \'J\'ai mangé\' (I ate), \'Elle est partie\' (she left). Build it with avoir OR être (in the present) + past participle: parler→parlé, finir→fini, vendre→vendu. The IMPARFAIT is for the background — what WAS happening, what USED TO happen, or a description in the past: \'Il faisait beau\' (the weather WAS nice), \'Je jouais au foot tous les jours\' (I USED TO play football every day). Endings: -ais, -ais, -ait, -ions, -iez, -aient. Rule of thumb: a single snapshot event = passé composé; a setting, habit or \'was -ing\' = imparfait. In a diary or story you weave them: \'Il pleuvait (imparfait, background) quand je suis sorti (passé composé, the event).\'',
