    gist: 'In French, words around a noun must MATCH it for gender (masculine/feminine) and number (singular/plural). Articles: le/un (masc.), la/une (fem.), les/des (plural) — \'une grande variété\', \'un grand jardin\'. Adjectives usually add -e for feminine and -s for plural: \'un petit chien\' → \'une petite maison\' → \'des petites maisons\'. Possessives match the THING owned, not the owner: \'ma grand-mère\' (feminine), \'mon frère\' (masculine), \'mes parents\' (plural) — so it\'s \'ma chambre\', never \'mon chambre\'. Many adjectives go AFTER the noun in French: \'une voiture rouge\', not \'une rouge voiture\'.',
