    gist: 'Concentration in moles per litre = moles of solute / volume in litres (so moles = concentration x volume in litres; remember cm3/1000 = litres). The key idea of DILUTION: adding water adds no solute, so the number of MOLES stays the same - only the volume (and therefore the concentration) changes. Worked example (SEC 2023 HL): 25.0 cm3 of a 0.12 M solution is diluted to 30.0 cm3 with deionised water; find the new concentration. Step 1 moles before = 0.12 x (25.0/1000) = 0.003 mol. Step 2 those same 0.003 mol now sit in 30.0 cm3, so new conc = 0.003 / (30.0/1000) = 0.003/0.030 = 0.10 mol/L. (Same result from C1V1 = C2V2: 0.12 x 25.0 = C2 x 30.0, C2 = 0.10 M.)',
