    focus: 'Charles\' law calculation (volume and temperature)', level: 'ordinary',
    gist: 'Charles\' law: at constant pressure, the volume of a fixed mass of gas is DIRECTLY PROPORTIONAL to its temperature on the KELVIN scale - so V1/T1 = V2/T2. The non-negotiable step is converting every Celsius temperature to kelvin by adding 273 BEFORE you put it into the formula. Worked example (SEC 2022 OL): a gas sample is 1.5 litres at 20 C; find its volume at 750 C at the same pressure. Step 1 convert: T1 = 20 + 273 = 293 K, T2 = 750 + 273 = 1023 K. Step 2 V2 = V1 x T2/T1 = 1.5 x 1023/293 = 5.2 litres. (Boyle\'s law is the partner rule: at constant temperature, volume is inversely proportional to pressure, P1V1 = P2V2.)',
