    focus: 'Kc calculation (ICE table)', level: 'higher',
    gist: 'For the reversible reaction PCl3(g) + Cl2(g) ⇌ PCl5(g), the equilibrium constant is Kc = [PCl5] ÷ ([PCl3][Cl2]) — square brackets mean equilibrium CONCENTRATIONS in mol l⁻¹, and they are ESSENTIAL for the mark. To calculate concentrations from starting amounts you build an Initial / Change / Equilibrium (ICE) line: every mole of PCl5 made uses one PCl3 and one Cl2, so if x mol l⁻¹ of PCl5 forms, PCl3 falls by x and Cl2 falls by x. Sub the equilibrium expressions into Kc, solve for x, then read off all three concentrations.',
    oneMove: {
      label: 'Set up the ICE line, form ONE equation in x, then solve and REJECT the impossible root',
      text: 'Start: [PCl3]=3.00, [Cl2]=1.20, [PCl5]=0. Change: −x, −x, +x. Equilibrium: (3.00−x), (1.20−x), x. So x ÷ [(3.00−x)(1.20−x)] = 2.5. This expands to a quadratic giving x = 1.00 or x = 3.6. You MUST reject x = 3.6 (it makes [Cl2] = 1.20 − 3.6 negative — impossible). So x = 1.00, giving [PCl3] = 2.0, [Cl2] = 0.2, [PCl5] = 1.0 mol l⁻¹. The scheme caps you at almost no marks (max 0,0,3,1) if you don\'t express all three in terms of x and form the quadratic.',
    },
    check: {
      prompt: 'PCl3(g) + Cl2(g) ⇌ PCl5(g), Kc = 2.5 at temperature T. Initially [PCl3] = 3.00 mol l⁻¹, [Cl2] = 1.20 mol l⁻¹ and no PCl5. (a) Write the Kc expression. (b) Calculate the equilibrium concentration of each substance.',
      modelAnswer: '(a) Kc = [PCl5] ÷ ([PCl3][Cl2]). (b) Let x = [PCl5] formed. Equilibrium: [PCl3] = 3.00−x, [Cl2] = 1.20−x, [PCl5] = x. So x / [(3.00−x)(1.20−x)] = 2.5, which gives the quadratic 2.5x² − 11.5x + 9 = 0, so x = 1.00 (reject x = 3.6, which gives a negative concentration). Therefore [PCl3] = 2.0 mol l⁻¹, [Cl2] = 0.2 mol l⁻¹ and [PCl5] = 1.0 mol l⁻¹. (Check: 1.0 ÷ (2.0 × 0.2) = 2.5. ✓)',
      needed: ['Kc = [PCl5] ÷ ([PCl3][Cl2]) with square brackets', 'ICE line: equilibrium amounts (3.00−x), (1.20−x), x', 'Solve the quadratic and reject x = 3.6', '[PCl3] = 2.0, [Cl2] = 0.2, [PCl5] = 1.0 mol l⁻¹'],
    },
    source: 'Topic 8.1 Chemical Equilibrium — SEC Leaving Certificate Chemistry 2023 Higher Level, Q9(b); marking scheme 2023 (square brackets essential; ICE line; x/[(3.00−x)(1.20−x)] = 2.5; x = 1.00 or 3.6; [PCl3]=2.0, [Cl2]=0.2, [PCl5]=1.0 mol/l). Figure: Q9(d) Le Châtelier graph (Cl2 added).',
    marksWeight: 18,
