    focus: 'Endo/exo from how Kc changes with temperature', level: 'higher',
    gist: 'An EXOthermic reaction gives out heat (ΔH negative); an ENDOthermic reaction takes in heat (ΔH positive). You can deduce which one a reversible reaction is from how its equilibrium constant Kc changes with temperature — a question students routinely get backwards. The logic: raising the temperature adds heat, and by Le Châtelier the equilibrium shifts in the direction that ABSORBS that heat (the endothermic direction). So whichever direction Kc favours MORE as you heat the system is the endothermic one. For 2NOBr(g) ⇌ 2NO(g) + Br2(g), Kc INCREASES with temperature — meaning heating pushes equilibrium toward the products (forward), so the forward (decomposition) reaction must be the endothermic one.',
