    gist: 'Combustion is the organic reaction type behind heat-of-combustion calculations. You find it from heats of formation using: dH(reaction) = sum of dH(formation) of products - sum of dH(formation) of reactants. Multiply each substance\'s heat of formation by its number in the balanced equation, keep the minus signs, then subtract the reactants total. Elements like O2 have a heat of formation of zero. Worked example (octane): for CH3(CH2)6CH3(l) + 12.5 O2(g) -> 8CO2(g) + 9H2O(l), with dHf(octane) = -250.3, dHf(CO2) = -393.5 and dHf(H2O) = -285.8 kJ/mol: products = 8(-393.5) + 9(-285.8) = -3148 + (-2572.2) = -5720.2; subtract the reactant = -5720.2 - (-250.3) = -5469.9 kJ/mol.',
