    gist: 'The fundamental frequency of a stretched string is f = (1/2L)*sqrt(T/mu), where L is the vibrating length, T is the tension and mu is the mass per unit length. The killer detail: mu = total mass / total length of the string, but L is only the distance between the two points the string is fixed/stretched across. These two lengths are often different, and using the wrong one for either quantity is the classic mark-loss. Rearrange for the unknown before substituting, and remember f is proportional to sqrt(T), so changing the tension by a factor scales f by the square root of that factor.',
