    id: 'phys-light-focal-length-converging-lens-procedure',
    subjectId: 'physics', topicId: 'physics-5-4',
    subjectLabel: 'Physics', topicLabel: 'Lenses',
    focus: 'Focal length of a converging lens (procedure + calculation)', level: 'higher',
    gist: 'This is the mandatory Section A experiment: measure the focal length f of a converging lens. You first get a quick approximate value, then take real u (object distance) and v (image distance) readings, and finally calculate f. Marks are lost on the \'approximate value\' step (people forget the distant-object method), on a vague procedure (not saying you move things until the image is SHARP), and in the calculation (using one data row instead of all four). Treat it as three scoring blocks: approximate value, full method, then 1/f = 1/u + 1/v averaged over every row.',
    oneMove: {
      label: 'Nail the approximate value: distant object onto a screen',
      text: 'The approximate-value step is worth easy marks and is the one most students get wrong or skip. The move: focus the image of a DISTANT object (e.g. a window or building, so rays are effectively parallel) onto a screen, then measure the distance from the lens to the screen with a metre stick — that distance is the approximate focal length. Don\'t describe the full u-v table here; that\'s a different step. Then for the real readings, always say you move the object/lens/screen until a SHARP (clearly focused) image forms before measuring v. For the calculation, use 1/f = 1/u + 1/v on EVERY row and average the four f values (or read f from a 1/v vs 1/u graph) — using just one row throws away marks.',
    },
    check: {
      prompt: 'In a mandatory experiment to measure the focal length f of a converging lens, a student first found an approximate value, then recorded these readings:\n  u (cm): 20.0, 30.0, 40.0, 50.0\n  v (cm): 78.7, 35.2, 26.9, 23.4\n(a) Describe how the student found the approximate value of f. (b) State how each value of v was determined and measured. (c) Using ALL the data, calculate the focal length of the lens.',
      modelAnswer: '(a) Focus the image of a distant object on a screen, then measure the distance from the screen (image) to the lens — this distance is the approximate focal length (because rays from a distant object are effectively parallel, so the image forms at the focal point).\n(b) Move the object, lens or screen until a sharp (clearly focused) image is formed on the screen, then measure the distance v from the lens to the screen using a metre stick.\n(c) Use the lens formula 1/f = 1/u + 1/v for each pair:\n  u=20.0, v=78.7: 1/f = 1/20.0 + 1/78.7 = 0.06271 -> f = 15.9 cm\n  u=30.0, v=35.2: 1/f = 1/30.0 + 1/35.2 = 0.06174 -> f = 16.2 cm\n  u=40.0, v=26.9: 1/f = 1/40.0 + 1/26.9 = 0.06217 -> f = 16.1 cm\n  u=50.0, v=23.4: 1/f = 1/50.0 + 1/23.4 = 0.06274 -> f = 15.9 cm\nAverage f = (15.9 + 16.2 + 16.1 + 15.9)/4 = 16.0 cm.',
      needed: ['Approximate value: focus the image of a DISTANT object on a screen, then measure the distance from the screen to the lens', 'Determining v: move the object/lens/screen until a SHARP image is formed, then measure v with a metre stick', 'Calculation: apply 1/f = 1/u + 1/v to each of the four rows and average the f values, giving f ~ 16.0 cm'],
    },
    source: 'Light: focal length of a converging lens (mandatory experiment) — SEC LC Physics 2024 Higher, Q2 + marking scheme (approximate value = focus distant object on screen and measure screen-to-lens distance; v measured by moving until sharp image, metre stick; f from 1/u+1/v averaged, f ~ 16.0 cm)',
    marksWeight: 40,
