      modelAnswer: 'Propaganda is the deliberate spreading of information designed to persuade an audience of a particular viewpoint, and Maisky shows clear skill at it. As the Soviet ambassador his PURPOSE is to defend his government, so he is not a neutral witness. He creates a sense of menace by labelling the accused a \'large terrorist conspiracy\' organised by \'the German Nazis\', framing the executions as defence rather than repression. He uses a real event - \'the death of Kirov\' - as \'striking proof\' to make the plot seem credible. He pre-empts criticism by conceding it is \'hard and unpleasant\' to shoot the plotters, then minimises 80-100 deaths against \'millions on the battlefield\'. The judgement rests on why and how he is arguing, not on whether the conspiracy was real.',
