    id: 'hist-eucharistic-congress-1932-ol',
    subjectId: 'history', topicId: 'history-2-11',
    subjectLabel: 'History', topicLabel: 'The Eucharistic Congress 1932',
    focus: 'Eucharistic Congress: events AND importance (Core Statements)', level: 'ordinary',
    gist: 'This Part C answer is marked by Core Statements worth 5 marks each, up to a Cumulative Mark of 30, plus up to 10 for the Overall Evaluation (max 40). The 2025 question asks what TOOK PLACE and its IMPORTANCE, so you need distinct points on both the events and why they mattered. Students lose marks by listing only crowd sizes and never explaining why the Congress was significant for the new Irish state.',
    oneMove: {
      label: 'Give separate Core Statements for the events and the importance',
      text: 'Each distinct relevant fact is one Core Statement (5 marks), so cover both halves: the EVENTS (Dublin hosted the 31st International Eucharistic Congress in June 1932; up to a million people gathered in the Phoenix Park for the open-air Mass; John McCormack sang Panis Angelicus; a Papal Legate attended and Pope Pius XI broadcast a radio message; G.K. Chesterton among visitors), and the IMPORTANCE (it showcased the new Free State to the world, displayed the close bond of Church and State under de Valera, and boosted national and Catholic identity and pride). Distinct points on both elements unlock the full 30 CM.',
    },
    check: {
      prompt: 'SEC LC History 2025 OL, Section 2, Part C, Q3: \'What took place during the Eucharistic Congress, 1932, and what was its importance?\' (40 marks). Write a model paragraph with distinct Core Statements on both the events and the importance.',
      modelAnswer: 'In June 1932 Dublin hosted the 31st International Eucharistic Congress, a major gathering of the Catholic Church, attended by a Papal Legate sent by Pope Pius XI. Huge crowds, estimated at up to a million people, attended the open-air Pontifical High Mass in the Phoenix Park, the central event of the week. The famous Irish tenor John McCormack sang \'Panis Angelicus\' during the Mass, and Pope Pius XI sent a special radio broadcast message to the gathering, while the streets and O\'Connell Bridge were decorated for the visitors. The Congress was important because it presented the young Irish Free State to the world as a confident, devoutly Catholic nation only ten years after the Civil War. It also showed the very close relationship between the Catholic Church and the State, with the new de Valera government taking part, and it greatly strengthened a sense of Catholic and national identity and pride among Irish people.',
      needed: ['Events: Dublin hosted the 31st International Eucharistic Congress in June 1932 with a Papal Legate (and Pope Pius XI\'s radio broadcast)', 'Events: up to a million people attended the open-air Mass in the Phoenix Park; John McCormack sang', 'Importance: it showcased the new Free State to the world and showed the close Church-State bond', 'Importance: it strengthened Catholic and national identity / pride'],
    },
    source: 'Topic: Case Study The Eucharistic Congress 1932 (within The pursuit of sovereignty and the impact of partition 1912-1949) — SEC LC History 2025 OL, Section 2 Ireland, Part C Q3 \'What took place during the Eucharistic Congress, 1932, and what was its importance?\' + marking scheme (Part C marked by Core Statement, each = 5 marks; Max CM = 30, Max OE = 10, total 40).',
    marksWeight: 40,
